{"meta":{"chapter_title":"AI in Education","title":"Generative AI and Student Work","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting generative ai and student work to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"03","section":"02","learning_objectives":["Distinguish product evidence from process evidence and explain why generative AI weakened product evidence.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Apply an allowed-use boundary to judge whether a student did the reserved work, rather than whether AI touched the document.","Critique AI-detector scores as probabilistic, equity-biased hypotheses rather than integrity evidence."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_generative_ai_and_student_work","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/03/01"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_3_2_generative_ai_and_student_work_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_3_2_generative_ai_and_student_work_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_3_2_generative_ai_and_student_work_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_3_2_generative_ai_and_student_work_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":2,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Generative AI and Student Work\n\n![Generative AI and Student Work textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/03-02-generative-ai-and-student-work.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe arrival of generative AI broke the oldest assumption in assessment: that a finished, fluent piece of writing is evidence that a student did the thinking. For decades, a polished essay was a reasonable proxy for learning, because producing polish required effort that overlapped with the learning itself. Generative AI severs that link. A student can now submit text they could not have produced and may not even understand, and it will look indistinguishable from mastery. That single change forces every teacher to rebuild how they read student work.\n\nThis section is about that rebuild. The wrong response, and the most common one, is to fight the new tools with detection software and harsher penalties, treating integrity as a policing problem. The professional response is to shift what you collect as evidence of learning, from the artifact alone to the process that produced it. A teacher who understands this stops asking \"did AI write this\" and starts asking \"can this student account for the thinking,\" which is both a fairer and a more answerable question.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Distinguish product evidence from process evidence and explain why generative AI weakened product evidence.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Apply an allowed-use boundary to judge whether a student did the reserved work, rather than whether AI touched the document.\n- Critique AI-detector scores as probabilistic, equity-biased hypotheses rather than integrity evidence.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA composition teacher runs every submission through an AI detector. One Monday it flags three essays as \"likely AI.\" She is about to file integrity reports when she looks at whose essays they are: all three are from her multilingual students, the ones who write in careful, formulaic sentences because they learned English structure before English idiom. None of her native-speaking students were flagged, even the two she suspects copied from a chatbot but who write loosely enough to read \"human.\"\n\nThe detector did not catch cheaters. It caught a writing style, and that style correlates with being an English learner. If she had acted on the scores, she would have punished her most vulnerable students for the crime of writing carefully while letting fluent native speakers slide. The detector did not give her evidence; it gave her a bias dressed as a number. The real question is what evidence would actually let her judge each case fairly, and the answer is not on the detector's dashboard.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe central shift in this section is from **product evidence** to **process evidence**. Product evidence is the finished artifact: the essay, the lab report, the code. Generative AI has made product evidence weakly diagnostic, because a fluent product no longer guarantees the learning behind it. Process evidence is the trail that shows thinking happening over time: drafts, outlines, version history, in-class writing, the student's ability to explain and extend their own work in conversation. AI can fake a product in seconds; it is far harder for a student to fake a four-day process they can also account for out loud.\n\nTwo ideas make this concrete and keep teachers out of trouble.\n\nFirst, **detectors are probabilistic classifiers, not evidence.** An AI detector outputs a likelihood, not a fact, and it produces that likelihood by recognizing statistical patterns in text. Crucially, those patterns are not \"wrongness\"; they are predictability. Writing that is simple, formulaic, or structurally regular reads as more \"AI-like,\" which is exactly how many multilingual students, younger writers, and students taught to follow templates write. The well-documented result is an elevated false-positive rate for these groups. A detector score is therefore a hypothesis with a known equity bias, never a finding, and it cannot be the basis of a consequence.\n\n**Recent evidence.** This is not hypothetical. Liang et al. (2023) tested seven widely used GPT detectors and found they misclassified 61.3% of TOEFL essays by non-native English writers as AI-generated, while classifying native-speaker essays almost perfectly as human; simply prompting to raise linguistic complexity removed the AI flag. OpenAI retired its own AI Text Classifier in July 2023 for a \"low rate of accuracy\" (it caught only about 26% of AI-generated text), which underscores that even the model vendor could not make detection reliable.\n\nSecond, **integrity is defined by the assignment's allowed-use boundary, not by the presence of AI.** \"Did the student use AI\" is the wrong question, because the answer is often \"yes, in a way you permitted.\" The right question is \"did the student do the work the assignment reserved for them.\" That requires you to have stated, in advance, which steps are theirs (the brainstorming, the drafting, the revision, the final reasoning) and which a tool may assist. Without that boundary, you have no standard to judge against, and any accusation is arbitrary.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A detector flags an essay at 88%. The same student has a four-day version history with two abandoned theses. Which is stronger evidence about whether the student did the reserved work?\" explanation=\"A detector outputs a probability with a known bias against formulaic and multilingual writers; it is a hypothesis, not a finding. A multi-day version history with abandoned drafts is process evidence that is far harder to fake and that directly shows the thinking happening over time.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The 88% detector score, because it is a precise number\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The version history, because process evidence is hard to fake and shows the thinking\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Neither; only an oral confession counts\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"They are equally strong\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n| Approach | What it actually measures | Why it fails or works |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Detector score as trigger | Text predictability | Penalizes formulaic and multilingual writers; not evidence |\n| Harsher penalties | Fear, not learning | Drives AI use underground; punishes the cautious |\n| Process evidence + oral check | Whether the student can account for the thinking | Hard to fake; fair across writing styles |\n| Clear allowed-use boundary | Did the student do the reserved work | Gives a real standard to judge against |\n\n## Worked Example\n\nTake a flat case and apply the framework. Two students submit nearly identical, fluent essays on the same topic. The detector scores both around 60%. A product-only reading treats them the same: both look polished, both look \"kind of AI.\"\n\nNow collect process evidence. Student A's document version history shows a three-day arc: a messy outline, two abandoned thesis statements, tracked revisions, and comments to herself. In a thirty-second hallway check she can explain why she dropped her second thesis. Student B's document was created in one eleven-minute session, pasted in whole, with no revision history, and in conversation he cannot say what his second body paragraph argues. Same product, same detector score, opposite process evidence. The integrity judgment is now obvious and defensible, and it never rested on the detector. The lesson generalizes: when you design assignments so process evidence accumulates (in-class drafting, version history, brief oral accounts), the AI-detection problem mostly dissolves, because faking the product no longer fakes the trail.\n\n## Worked Decision: Responding to a Detector Flag\n\nFollow a teacher's annotated before/after judgment on a suspected-AI essay. The student, Mateo, is a multilingual writer; the detector reports \"82% likely AI.\"\n\n1. **Before (initial impulse):** \"The detector says 82%, so Mateo used AI. I will give a zero and report it.\" The only evidence is the percentage.\n2. **Constraint named:** Annotate the tool's limits. Detectors are probabilistic and show elevated false-positive rates for multilingual and formulaic writers. An 82% score is a guess, not a finding.\n3. **Process evidence gathered:** Annotate what the student work shows. Mateo's planning doc lists three AI-suggested topics, which the assignment explicitly allowed. His draft history shows five revisions over four days, and his outline matches his final argument.\n4. **After (revised decision):** \"I will not penalize for AI use. The allowed step was brainstorming and his draft history shows his own revision work. I will keep a short oral check in my next conference to confirm he can reconstruct the argument, and I will stop treating the detector score as a trigger for consequences.\"\n5. **Evidence trail:** The revision is justified by the assignment's allowed-use boundary and by process artifacts, not by a detector number. The rejected move, acting on the percentage, is recorded with its reason.\n\nStep 1 to step 4 shows the section's core lesson: integrity judgments rest on process evidence and the assignment's defined boundary, not on a probabilistic score or on prose polish.\n\nNow walk the same case as the decision-maker. You are Mateo's teacher and the detector just returned \"82% likely AI.\" Each branch tests whether you let the score drive a consequence or gather process evidence first.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Responding to a detector flag on a multilingual writer\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"The detector reports 82 percent likely AI on Mateo, a multilingual writer. What do you do first?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"File an integrity report; 82 percent is high\",\"next\":\"report\",\"feedback\":\"Detectors are probabilistic and biased against multilingual and formulaic writers. 82 percent is a guess, not a finding.\"},{\"label\":\"Treat the score as a hypothesis and check the allowed-use boundary\",\"next\":\"boundary\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. The first question is what the assignment actually reserved for the student.\"},{\"label\":\"Ignore it entirely; detectors are useless\",\"next\":\"ignore\",\"feedback\":\"The flag can prompt a fair review; it just cannot be the evidence by itself.\"}]},\"report\":{\"prompt\":\"Before you submit, you remember the false-positive bias against multilingual writers. Reconsider?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Pull the draft history and the allowed-use boundary first\",\"next\":\"boundary\",\"feedback\":\"You stepped back from acting on a biased number.\"}]},\"ignore\":{\"prompt\":\"You still owe a fair check. Where do you look for real evidence?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Version history, planning doc, and a brief oral account\",\"next\":\"boundary\",\"feedback\":\"Process evidence, gathered against the stated boundary.\"}]},\"boundary\":{\"prompt\":\"The assignment explicitly allowed AI brainstorming. The planning doc lists three AI-suggested topics; his draft shows five revisions over four days. What is your decision?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No penalty; the allowed step was used and the process evidence is his own\",\"next\":\"fair\",\"feedback\":\"Defensible. The reserved work was done; the detector never decided it.\"},{\"label\":\"Penalize anyway because AI touched the document\",\"next\":\"unfair\",\"feedback\":\"Any permitted AI use is not a violation; this punishes allowed behavior.\"}]},\"fair\":{\"prompt\":\"Defensible outcome: you judged whether the reserved work was done, kept a short oral check for the next conference, and stopped treating the score as a trigger.\",\"choices\":[]},\"unfair\":{\"prompt\":\"Non-defensible outcome: you punished a multilingual student for using an allowed step, based on a biased probability.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n**Hidden cost of the process-evidence move.** Submitting student work to a commercial detector or generative-AI service is itself a data-disclosure decision: the essays are education records (FERPA), under-13 contexts add COPPA, whose amended rule was finalized in 2025 with compliance through 2026, and version-history-as-evidence is a form of process surveillance whose burden falls unevenly on students with less private time, hardware, or connectivity. Choose process evidence with the same datafication lens you point at the detector.\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen a piece of student work raises an AI-integrity concern, never let a detector score drive a consequence. Instead:\n\n1. **Treat the detector as a hypothesis, not evidence**, and remember its bias against formulaic and multilingual writers.\n2. **Check the allowed-use boundary you set in advance.** If the assignment permitted the step in question (brainstorming, outlining), there is no violation to investigate.\n3. **Gather process evidence**: version history, drafts, in-class work, and a brief oral account where the student explains and extends their own reasoning.\n4. **Judge whether the student did the reserved work**, not whether AI touched the document.\n\nAct only when the process evidence, not the percentage, supports the conclusion. The burden of evidence rises with the severity of the consequence.\n\nPut the rule in the right order. Arrange these steps into the defensible sequence a teacher follows after an AI-integrity concern is raised, from first move to last.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Treat any detector score as a hypothesis, not evidence\",\"Check the allowed-use boundary you set in advance\",\"Gather process evidence: version history, drafts, in-class work\",\"Hold a brief oral account where the student explains and extends the work\",\"Judge whether the student did the reserved work, then act\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is \"the detector said 90%, so it is proven,\" which mistakes a probability with a known bias for a fact. The second is the reverse overcorrection: assuming any AI involvement is cheating, which punishes students who used AI exactly as you allowed and pushes everyone else to hide their use. The third is believing the solution is better detection software; detection is an arms race teachers lose, while assignment design that generates process evidence is a position you can hold. The deepest misreading is treating this as a discipline problem at all. It is an assessment-design problem: the question is what evidence of learning you collect, and AI simply exposed that the old proxy (a clean product) was always thin.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nWalk the framework through a realistic redesign. A biology teacher's lab reports started coming back suspiciously uniform and fluent. Her first instinct was a detector; the framework points her elsewhere.\n\nShe starts from the learning the report is supposed to evidence: interpreting their own data and connecting it to the mechanism. A chatbot can write a generic report on photosynthesis, but it cannot interpret the specific, slightly-off numbers from this group's failed trial. So she redesigns the assignment to anchor it in process and local evidence: students submit their raw data table and a photo of the setup, write the interpretation section in a timed in-class block, and answer one targeted question (\"why did your group's trial diverge from the prediction\") that only their actual experience can answer.\n\nNow the allowed-use boundary is explicit: AI may help polish grammar and check vocabulary, but the interpretation must be the student's, produced in class, about their own data. Integrity is no longer a detector verdict; it is visible in whether the interpretation matches the group's real results. A student who outsourced the report would write a fluent but generic interpretation that does not fit the divergent data, and the mismatch, not a percentage, is the evidence. She solved an integrity problem by changing what counted as the work.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader of this topic refuses to evaluate student work, or AI policy, on the product alone. They ask what process evidence exists and whether the assignment was designed to generate any. As you read, keep a two-column note with the **claim about a student's AI use** on the left and the **process evidence that would confirm or refute it** on the right. If the only thing in the right column is a detector score, the case is not yet decidable.\n\nA strong analysis can answer four questions about any suspected-AI case:\n\n1. What was the assignment's allowed-use boundary, stated in advance?\n2. What process evidence exists, and what does it actually show?\n3. Does the detector score, if any, carry a known bias against this student's group?\n4. What assignment redesign would make this judgment easy next time without detection software?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour tension map is strong when it names the real conflict in this topic (efficient detection versus fair evidence) and resolves it with a process-evidence design rather than a policing tool. Show the allowed-use boundary you would set, the process evidence you would collect, and the equity reason you reject detector-driven consequences. The weakest maps say \"use AI detectors carefully\"; the strongest say \"I will not act on detector scores because of their bias against multilingual writers, and instead I will design assignments so process evidence makes the judgment fair.\"\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact would read: \"I rejected detector scores as a trigger because they false-flag my formulaic and multilingual writers; I redesigned the task so version history and a thirty-second oral account, which are far harder to fake, carry the integrity judgment instead.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the problem to a math classroom, where \"writing\" is not the work. A student submits a perfect, fully worked problem set. There is no prose for a detector to score, and the answers are simply right or wrong. The product-versus-process distinction still governs: the process evidence becomes the student's ability to solve a structurally similar problem on a whiteboard, or to explain why a step is valid. The specific evidence changes from version history to live problem-solving, but the principle is identical: a correct product no longer proves the learning, so you collect evidence of the thinking instead. Getting to the right evidence in a setting with no text to detect is the test that you understand the principle, not just the essay-classroom version of it.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_ai_in_education\",\"cat531_supplement_generative_ai_and_student_work\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/03-02-generative-ai-and-student-work-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"03.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 03.02. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"1\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_generative_ai_and_student_work\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace records evidence source, risk, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates professional judgment from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- Mishra, P., & Koehler, M. J. (2006). Technological pedagogical content knowledge: A framework for teacher knowledge. Teachers College Record, 108(6), 1017-1054. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Ning, Y., et al. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. Sustainability, 16(3), 978. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- Liang, W., Yuksekgonul, M., Mao, Y., Wu, E., & Zou, J. (2023). GPT detectors are biased against non-native English writers. Patterns, 4(7), 100779. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patter.2023.100779\n- Sullivan, M., Kelly, A., & McLaughlan, P. (2023). ChatGPT in higher education: Considerations for academic integrity and student learning. Journal of Applied Learning & Teaching, 6(1). https://doi.org/10.37074/jalt.2023.6.1.17\n- Bower, M., Torrington, J., Lai, J. W. M., Petocz, P., & Alfano, M. (2024). How should we change teaching and assessment in response to increasingly powerful generative artificial intelligence? Education and Information Technologies, 29, 15403-15439. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10639-023-12405-0\n- Selwyn, N. (2016). Distrusting Educational Technology: Critical Questions for Changing Times. Routledge. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2019). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. Learning, Media and Technology, 45(3), 251-269. https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_2_generative_ai_and_student_work_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_generative_ai_and_student_work","stem":"A turnitin-style detector reports an essay is \"82% likely AI-generated.\" The student is a recent immigrant who writes in a careful, formulaic style. What is the most defensible next step?","options":["Assign a zero, since 82% is a high probability.","Treat the score as a flag, not a verdict, and meet with the student to talk through her drafts and outline.","Lower the grade by 82% to match the detector.","Report the student to the office immediately based on the percentage."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Detectors are probabilistic and show known false positives for multilingual writers. A percentage cannot establish dishonesty; process evidence and a conversation can.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_2_detector_as_proof","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_generative_ai_and_student_work","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_2_generative_ai_and_student_work_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_generative_ai_and_student_work","stem":"An assignment explicitly allows students to use AI to brainstorm topic ideas but requires them to draft and revise the essay themselves. A student lists three AI-suggested topics in his planning doc, then writes the essay alone. How should the teacher judge this?","options":["It is cheating, because he used AI at all.","It is acceptable use, because brainstorming was the allowed AI step and the drafting was his own.","It is cheating, because AI touched the assignment.","It cannot be judged without an AI detector."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Integrity is defined by the assignment's allowed-use boundary. The student stayed inside the permitted use, so this is assisted work, not dishonesty.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_2_any_ai_use_is_cheating","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_generative_ai_and_student_work","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_2_generative_ai_and_student_work_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_generative_ai_and_student_work","stem":"A teacher wants assessment that stays meaningful in a generative-AI world. Which assignment design best surfaces a student's own thinking?","options":["A take-home essay graded only on the final polished text.","A take-home essay plus a required annotated outline, draft history, and a brief in-class reflection on what AI did and did not contribute.","A multiple-choice quiz auto-graded overnight.","A single fluency score generated by the writing platform."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Collecting process artifacts makes both learning and AI shortcuts visible. Grading only the final product hides how the work was produced.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_2_product_only_assessment","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_generative_ai_and_student_work","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_2_generative_ai_and_student_work_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_generative_ai_and_student_work","stem":"A student submits a flawless, fluent lab report. During a 60-second oral check, she cannot explain why her control group mattered or what her own graph shows. What does this most defensibly indicate, and what should the teacher do?","options":["The fluent writing proves understanding, so award full marks.","Fluent prose can mask missing comprehension; the teacher should weight the oral evidence and reteach the experimental-design reasoning.","The oral check is irrelevant because the written report is polished.","She should be reported for cheating based on the polish alone."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Generative AI produces fluency without comprehension. A quick oral probe reveals whether the student can reconstruct her own reasoning, which is the learning signal that matters.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_3_2_fluent_means_understood","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_ai_in_education","cat531_supplement_generative_ai_and_student_work","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_2_detector_as_proof","pattern":"ai_detector_score_treated_as_proof","description":"Treating an AI-text-detector percentage as proof that a student cheated","trigger":"teacher issues an academic-integrity consequence based on a detector score alone","feedback":"AI detectors produce probabilistic guesses with documented false positives, especially for multilingual and neurodivergent writers. Use process evidence, drafts, and a conversation, not a percentage, to make an integrity judgment.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_2_any_ai_use_is_cheating","pattern":"all_generative_ai_use_equated_with_cheating","description":"Assuming every use of generative AI on student work is academic dishonesty regardless of the assignment's allowed use","trigger":"teacher penalizes brainstorming or grammar help that the assignment permitted","feedback":"Define allowed, assisted, and prohibited uses for the specific assignment before judging. Cheating is doing what the task forbade, not using a tool the task allowed.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_2_product_only_assessment","pattern":"grading_only_the_final_product","description":"Assessing only the polished final text, which makes generative-AI shortcuts invisible and unteachable","trigger":"teacher collects only the finished essay with no drafts, notes, or process artifacts","feedback":"Collect process evidence: outlines, annotated drafts, and a short reflection on what AI did and did not do. Process makes both learning and shortcuts visible.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_3_2_fluent_means_understood","pattern":"fluent_ai_prose_mistaken_for_understanding","description":"Reading polished AI-assisted prose as evidence that the student understands the content","trigger":"teacher assigns a high grade to fluent work without checking for the student's own reasoning","feedback":"Generative AI produces fluent text without comprehension. Probe with a quick oral question or an in-class write to see whether the student can reconstruct the reasoning.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"df3e03050c548ff7bfdeac41ca3a9ffd3becefb5530dfdc05485466c9ffe169c","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}